---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import homeHero from "../assets/images/home-hero.jpg";
import Timeline from "../components/Timeline.astro";
import Planner from "../components/Planner.astro";
import WalkthroughButton from "../components/WalkthroughButton.astro";
import { courseMeta, helpful, prerequisites, sequel } from "../course-config";
import { midSemesterBreak, teachingEnds } from "../lib/calendar";
import { constraints } from "../lib/model";
import { homes, measurement, money } from "../lib/cases";
import { clientImageAlt, clientImages } from "../lib/client-images";
import { formatCourseDate } from "../lib/dates";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={homeHero}
  heroImageAlt="An engineer reviews a suburban home, rainwater tank, garden beds and composting systems."
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="claim">
    What can this client's land <a href={withBase("/lectures/week-09/")}>reliably provide</a>, and
    which improvements <a href={withBase("/lectures/week-11/")}>justify their cost</a>?
  </p>

  <p>
    That is the question a homeowner asks when they apply for a loan to make their house more
    self-reliant, and it is the one this course trains you to answer. You work as a graduate
    engineer at a small consultancy, taking one household from its first meeting to a costed
    proposal. <strong>The government is imaginary. Your water balance still has to add up.</strong>
  </p>

  <div id="start-here" class="start">
    <a class="start-primary" href={withBase("/weeks/week-01/")}>Start with week 1</a>
    <a class="start-secondary" href={withBase("/clients/")}>Choose a client home</a>
  </div>

  <WalkthroughButton page="home" />

  <dl class="orient" id="orientation">
    <dt>What this course is</dt>
    <dd>
      A twelve-week engineering design course. You are given one real Australian climate, one
      fixed existing house and one fictional client, and you decide what their household should
      spend to become more self-reliant in food, water and waste. The
      <a href={withBase("/method/#the-scenario")}>loan programme and the consultancy</a> are
      fiction; the climates, the water balances, the soil, the pumps and the prices are not.
    </dd>
    <dt id="what-you-submit">What you submit</dt>
    <dd>
      Ten small tutorial checkpoints (20 %), five short quizzes (20 %), a measurement programme in
      week 7 (20 %), and an investment proposal in the assessment period (40 %).
      <a href={withBase("/assessments/")}>Assessment</a> has the criteria; half the marks are for
      the weekly work the two assignments are assembled from.
    </dd>
    <dt>What to do this week</dt>
    <dd>
      Open <a href={withBase("/weeks/")}>the weekly map</a>, find your week, and read the two
      paragraphs at the top of it. If this is your first visit, start at
      <a href={withBase("/weeks/week-01/")}>week 1</a>: choose a client home and build the
      evidence workbook every later week writes into.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="three-groups">How this site is arranged</h2>
  <p>
    Three groups, in the navigation above and in the menu on a phone. Knowing which group you are
    in tells you whether something is a task or a reference.
  </p>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <Card title="Weekly study">
      <p>
        What you do each week. <a href={withBase("/weeks/")}>Weeks</a> first — it is the map, and
        the other three are entry points into the same material:
        <a href={withBase("/lectures/")}>Lectures</a>,
        <a href={withBase("/tutorials/")}>{sessionLabels.plural}</a> and
        <a href={withBase("/assessments/")}>Assessments</a>.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Course reference">
      <p>
        Material you come back to all semester, not another weekly task list. The
        <a href={withBase("/clients/")}>five client homes</a>, and
        <a href={withBase("/method/")}>Programme &amp; method</a> — the scenario, the definitions,
        the bands, the price schedule and the datasets.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={"People & policies"}>
      <p>
        Who teaches what and how to reach them (<a href={withBase("/people/")}>People</a>), and
        deadlines, extensions, generative AI and how the fiction and the facts are kept apart
        (<a href={withBase("/policies/")}>Policies</a>).
      </p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>

  <h2>One commission, two assignments</h2>
  <p>
    First you work out what you would need to know. From an intake dossier and a scaled plan of an
    existing home you design a year of measurement: which instruments, where, how often, how
    accurate, what it costs against a {money(measurement.allowance)} allowance, and how every reading
    feeds a decision. That is Assignment 1, due in week 7.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then the story jumps twelve months. A specialist team hands over what the measurement year
    found, and you compare the existing home with three feasible retrofit options and recommend
    one: the cheapest arrangement that meets the household's own service targets, then an honest
    case for any money spent beyond it. That is Assignment 2, the technical annex to the family's
    loan application.
  </p>
  <p class="under">
    Nobody applies for a real loan and nobody waits a real year. Everything you calculate before
    week 7's handover is a scoping estimate on published figures, labelled as one.
  </p>

  <Planner />

  <Timeline />
  <p class="under">
    Every tile is a <a href={withBase("/weeks/")}>week overview</a>: its question, its lecture,
    its tutorial, what is assessed in it, and what you leave holding.
  </p>

  <h2>The five clients</h2>
  <p>
    Every student advises one of five fixed existing homes in five Australian climates. There is no
    sixth option and no custom site: the comparison between homes is part of what the course teaches.
  </p>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    {homes.map((h) => (
      <Card
        title={`${h.short}: the ${h.family} household`}
        href={`/clients/${h.slug}/`}
        image={clientImages[h.slug]}
        imageAlt={clientImageAlt[h.slug]}
      >
        <p>{h.dossier.priorities[0]}</p>
        <p class="art-label">Conceptual illustration · scaled plans govern.</p>
      </Card>
    ))}
  </CardGrid>

  <h2>What the engineering is</h2>
  <p>
    A constrained, multi-objective decision made with spreadsheet-sized models you can check by
    hand. Seven decision variables — storage, which roof zones to connect, how much ground to crop
    and with what, how to use rainwater and greywater, which biological modules to adopt, and how
    to run it all through the year — each with a short list of options bounded by what the home
    already has. Two hard limits from the programme: {money(constraints.budget_aud)} including
    contingency, and {constraints.energy_cap_kwh_day} kWh a day in the peak month.
  </p>
  <p>
    You will size storage from a monthly balance, estimate a critical flow and a pump head, check
    nutrient recovery against what the crop can take up, and test your recommendation against a
    dry year. You will not pick pipe diameters, draw wiring or certify anything; you specify
    performance and how a builder proves it has been met.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will not do</h2>
  <p>
    You will not build, operate, eat from or drink from anything, and you will not collect a year
    of real data. Where the course sets a safety rule stricter than the law in your client's
    jurisdiction, it says so, and says why.
  </p>
  <p>
    Energy supply and shelter are out of scope. Biogas is recorded and not credited; only the
    digestate's nutrients count. Those belong to <strong>{sequel.code} — {sequel.title}</strong>,
    a {sequel.status} that adds {sequel.adds}.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>Fourth-year engineering students. You should arrive with:</p>
  <ul>
    {prerequisites.map((p) => <li>{p}</li>)}
  </ul>
  <p>{helpful}</p>

  <h2>The semester</h2>
  <p>
    Twelve teaching weeks from {formatCourseDate(courseMeta.startDate)} to{" "}
    {formatCourseDate(teachingEnds)}, with a two-week mid-semester break from{" "}
    {formatCourseDate(midSemesterBreak.from)}. Assignment 2 falls in the assessment period, which
    is why the course record runs to {formatCourseDate(courseMeta.endDate)}.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="The twelve weeks" href="/weeks/">
      <p>The weekly map: what each week asks, what it teaches, and what you leave holding.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={"Programme & method"} href="/method/">
      <p>The fictional loan programme and the consultancy, then every definition, band, price and dataset.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>One engineering question a week, a named method, and a worked example at that week's home.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/tutorials/">
      <p>Ninety-minute sessions with worked examples on a practice house and exercises on your own home.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>Ten tutorial checkpoints, five conceptual quizzes, the measurement programme and the investment proposal.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Measurement primer" href="/method/measurement/">
      <p>What a household measurement programme measures, in what units, how often and how well.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Policies" href="/policies/">
      <p>Deadlines, extensions, generative AI, and how the fiction and the facts are kept apart.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .orient {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(9rem, 12rem) 1fr;
    gap: 0.6rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }

  .orient dt {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .orient dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .under {
    margin-top: -0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .orient {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.2rem;
    }

    .orient dd {
      margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
    }
  }

  .start {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
  }

  .start a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 2.75rem;
    padding: 0.55rem 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-primary);
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-weight: 650;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .start-primary {
    background: var(--at-primary);
    color: var(--at-bg);
  }

  .start-secondary {
    color: var(--at-text);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }

  .start a:hover,
  .start a:focus-visible {
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-primary) 28%, transparent);
  }

  .art-label {
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--at-text-muted);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
  }

  .claim {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--at-primary);
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  :global(.at-hero-image) {
    object-position: 68% center;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    :global(.at-hero) {
      min-height: 31rem;
    }

    :global(.at-hero-image) {
      object-position: 67% center;
    }

    :global(.at-hero-title) {
      font-size: clamp(2rem, 10vw, 2.7rem);
    }

    .start a {
      justify-content: center;
      width: 100%;
    }
  }
</style>
